---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Privacy — sudonotes docs"
  description="What sudonotes sends over the network, what it never sends, what the AI proxy logs, and how to switch it all off."
  type="article"
  breadcrumbs={[{ label: "Docs", href: "/docs" }, { label: "Privacy" }]}
>
  <section class="page prose" style="padding-block: clamp(44px, 7vw, 76px)">
    <p class="eyebrow">Docs</p>
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      sudonotes has no accounts and no sync. Your notes live in a folder on your machine. There are
      exactly three things that use the network, and all of them are listed here.
    </p>

    <h2>1. AI assistance — on by default, per vault</h2>
    <p>
      When it is on, these leave your machine and go to the sudonotes API at
      <code>sudonotes.com</code>, which forwards them to DeepSeek:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>the note's <strong>title and full body</strong>;</li>
      <li>whether it is a prompt or an idea;</li>
      <li>public catalog metadata about the model you assigned, if any.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      That happens when a note is auto-tagged after a save, when you paste text that needs a title,
      and when you press <strong>Review this note</strong>. Automatic tagging is throttled: a note
      must have real content, must have changed by more than a typo, and must not have been tagged
      in the last minute.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Turn it off and none of that happens.</strong> The switch is in the right-hand panel
      and is stored in <code>.sudonotes/settings.json</code> inside the vault, so two vaults can
      answer differently. With it off, tagging falls back to a local keyword pass and nothing is
      sent anywhere.
    </p>

    <h2>What the proxy keeps</h2>
    <p>
      The proxy exists so you do not need an API key of your own. It is a
      <a href="https://github.com/mory-dev/sudonotes/tree/master/worker">Cloudflare Worker in this
      repository</a> — you can read exactly what it does.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Note content is never logged.</strong> Logs contain the model name, token counts and cost. That is all.</li>
      <li><strong>Your IP is not stored.</strong> Rate limiting uses a keyed hash of it, which cannot be reversed back to an address.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>The device id is random and anonymous.</strong> It is generated on first use, holds
        no information about you, and exists so limits can be per install rather than per IP.
      </li>
      <li><strong>No accounts, so nothing to correlate.</strong> Requests are not tied to an identity.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Free means free to you, not unmetered. There are per-device and per-IP request limits and a
      daily spend ceiling. When a limit is hit the app quietly falls back to local tagging.
    </p>

    <h2>2. The model catalog</h2>
    <p>
      The model picker fetches a public catalog from <code>models.dev</code> once a day and caches
      it. It is a plain list of models and their capabilities. Nothing about your notes is included
      in that request.
    </p>

    <h2>3. Nothing else</h2>
    <p>
      No telemetry, no crash reporting, no update pings, no analytics inside the app. The desktop
      app makes no other outbound request.
    </p>

    <h2>This website</h2>
    <p>
      Static pages, no cookies, no third-party scripts, no fonts loaded from anyone else's server.
      There is currently no analytics on this site at all. If that changes it will be Cloudflare Web
      Analytics, which is cookie-free and page-level — and this paragraph will change with it.
      Search queries, note titles, vault paths and prompt content are never collected, by any
      mechanism, ever.
    </p>

    <div class="note">
      If you want certainty rather than a promise: the app is MIT licensed and the proxy is in the
      same repository. The list above is checkable.
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
